WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.196 --> 00:00:07.836
All right, so I'm going
to show you how to get

00:00:07.836 --> 00:00:10.606
started working on
Shiny for Python apps.

00:00:10.606 --> 00:00:12.576
So this is our
website, at least,

00:00:12.576 --> 00:00:14.436
the website in
its current state.

00:00:14.436 --> 00:00:16.066
It will be changing
in the future.

00:00:16.066 --> 00:00:20.586
You don't have to install Python
and install Shiny and set up

00:00:20.586 --> 00:00:22.116
you virtual end
and all that stuff

00:00:22.116 --> 00:00:26.556
that you normally do for
developing Python code.

00:00:26.556 --> 00:00:28.919
You can just go to our
website and click on examples.

00:00:28.919 --> 00:00:30.336
There's some
examples button here.

00:00:30.336 --> 00:00:32.286
Or it's in the bar up here.

00:00:32.286 --> 00:00:35.496
So I just click
on that, and this

00:00:35.496 --> 00:00:40.086
takes you right into this online
Shiny for Python app editor.

00:00:40.086 --> 00:00:43.233
So we've got this app code
here, and it's running

00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:44.316
the application over here.

00:00:44.316 --> 00:00:48.366
And I can make changes to
this and say, "Hi Everyone!"

00:00:50.886 --> 00:00:52.596
And I click this button.

00:00:52.596 --> 00:00:54.736
We run it, and the app
is running right here.

00:00:54.736 --> 00:00:57.126
Now, the really cool
thing is that this is all

00:00:57.126 --> 00:01:00.046
running in the web browser.

00:01:00.046 --> 00:01:02.136
So this isn't
running on a server,

00:01:02.136 --> 00:01:04.971
and you don't have to worry that
if you change this code here,

00:01:04.971 --> 00:01:06.846
it's going to change
something on our server.

00:01:06.846 --> 00:01:10.966
This is all running on your
computer in your web browser.

00:01:10.966 --> 00:01:12.876
So you can feel
free to experiment

00:01:12.876 --> 00:01:15.286
with things as much as you want.

00:01:15.286 --> 00:01:18.906
Now, over here, there's a whole
bunch of different examples

00:01:18.906 --> 00:01:20.116
that you can look at.

00:01:20.116 --> 00:01:22.506
So it just starts off in
this really basic one.

00:01:22.506 --> 00:01:24.786
It has a slider
and a text output.

00:01:24.786 --> 00:01:28.426
I can move the slider, and
some of the stuff here changes.

00:01:28.426 --> 00:01:31.911
But if you want to see
how to do other things

00:01:31.911 --> 00:01:33.456
and explore other
features of Shiny,

00:01:33.456 --> 00:01:36.076
you can look at the
examples from the side.

00:01:36.076 --> 00:01:39.516
So another basic example
is an app with a plot.

00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:41.331
And it's asking me
here, like, hey, you

00:01:41.331 --> 00:01:43.206
want to discard all the
changes to the files?

00:01:43.206 --> 00:01:45.646
Because I edited this
app a little bit.

00:01:45.646 --> 00:01:48.089
But since I'm switching
to another example,

00:01:48.089 --> 00:01:49.506
I'm just going to
throw that away.

00:01:49.506 --> 00:01:54.066
And hit-- again, that won't
hurt anything anywhere.

00:01:54.066 --> 00:01:56.596
In case, you wanted to keep
the changes on your computer,

00:01:56.596 --> 00:01:58.186
that's why it's asking me.

00:01:58.186 --> 00:02:01.836
So here is a map with a
plot, and I move a slider,

00:02:01.836 --> 00:02:05.916
and this histogram changes
a number of things.

00:02:05.916 --> 00:02:09.276
And so you know, you can
make this a little wider.

00:02:09.276 --> 00:02:12.136
The layout changes when
it's really narrow.

00:02:12.136 --> 00:02:15.186
So this is a sidebar layout.

00:02:15.186 --> 00:02:19.776
And normally, when the
window is wide enough,

00:02:19.776 --> 00:02:22.546
it'll show the sidebar
content on the side.

00:02:22.546 --> 00:02:25.656
But if you make it narrow, then
it flows, so it'll be on top.

00:02:25.656 --> 00:02:28.306
And so it will be useful
on mobile devices.

00:02:28.306 --> 00:02:30.626
We can look other-- there's
other examples here.

00:02:30.626 --> 00:02:32.586
I'm just going to fly
through some of these.

00:02:32.586 --> 00:02:35.476
Just to get some idea of
the things that you can do.

00:02:35.476 --> 00:02:39.366
So you can have an app
that lets you upload files.

00:02:39.366 --> 00:02:44.736
And this shows the code for
how to do an upload, downloads.

00:02:44.736 --> 00:02:46.461
I'll skip down to
some of the inputs.

00:02:49.056 --> 00:02:51.996
So there's a lot of input and
output components for Shiny.

00:02:51.996 --> 00:02:53.326
So this is a text input.

00:02:53.326 --> 00:02:57.666
And if I type some
text here, so there.

00:02:57.666 --> 00:03:01.986
This input_text gets used
by the server code here,

00:03:01.986 --> 00:03:05.461
and it constructs the
string and returns that.

00:03:05.461 --> 00:03:07.086
And then, that gets
rendered over here.

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:08.586
All right, so there's
other-- again,

00:03:08.586 --> 00:03:11.556
there's other types of inputs
like the numeric inputs,

00:03:11.556 --> 00:03:16.956
similar to the text input,
sliders, checkboxes.

00:03:16.956 --> 00:03:20.766
If I check it, that would
become true or false.

00:03:23.796 --> 00:03:25.456
Checkbox groups.

00:03:25.456 --> 00:03:27.816
So I can select multiple
items, and it'll

00:03:27.816 --> 00:03:31.356
give me a tuple with
all the selected items.

00:03:34.516 --> 00:03:38.586
A Select Input, so
like a dropdown box.

00:03:38.586 --> 00:03:44.996
Radio buttons,
text areas, so this

00:03:44.996 --> 00:03:48.386
is for multi-line text input.

00:03:48.386 --> 00:03:53.976
And there's date
inputs and date ranges.

00:03:53.976 --> 00:03:57.116
And password.

00:03:57.116 --> 00:03:59.726
It will hide it in the
browser, but that value

00:03:59.726 --> 00:04:01.948
gets sent to the server.

00:04:01.948 --> 00:04:03.156
So that's some of the inputs.

00:04:03.156 --> 00:04:06.126
So for the outputs--

00:04:06.126 --> 00:04:07.886
this is actually the
same app as the input

00:04:07.886 --> 00:04:10.436
because you type in
something, and Then.

00:04:10.436 --> 00:04:13.016
It sends it back to the--
it sends output back

00:04:13.016 --> 00:04:14.976
to the browser.

00:04:14.976 --> 00:04:16.736
So there's actually
two varieties of this.

00:04:16.736 --> 00:04:18.266
There's the text
output, and this

00:04:18.266 --> 00:04:21.896
is normal HTML text, the
verbatim text output,

00:04:21.896 --> 00:04:24.356
where returns it as code.

00:04:24.356 --> 00:04:28.796
Or sorry, code formatted text.

00:04:28.796 --> 00:04:34.076
There's UI outputs, which let
you display any user interface

00:04:34.076 --> 00:04:35.606
components as HTML.

00:04:35.606 --> 00:04:38.636
This component here,
this text input,

00:04:38.636 --> 00:04:41.276
is actually output generated
dynamically in the server

00:04:41.276 --> 00:04:42.896
and then sent to the browser.

00:04:42.896 --> 00:04:45.896
So that's the UI
that's being sent over.

00:04:48.506 --> 00:04:51.336
And plot outputs.

00:04:51.336 --> 00:04:53.576
So if you need to
display tables,

00:04:53.576 --> 00:04:56.941
currently, you can
use a UI output.

00:04:56.941 --> 00:04:58.316
If you've got a
pandas DataFrame,

00:04:58.316 --> 00:05:00.806
you can have it generate
a table with a two HTML

00:05:00.806 --> 00:05:02.276
method on those data frames.

00:05:02.276 --> 00:05:04.776
But hopefully, in
the near future,

00:05:04.776 --> 00:05:07.349
we'll have a proper
render table output.

00:05:07.349 --> 00:05:08.891
There's other fun
little things here,

00:05:08.891 --> 00:05:11.276
like different-- there's
a couple different layout

00:05:11.276 --> 00:05:15.236
examples and some examples of
how to use various reactive

00:05:15.236 --> 00:05:15.926
components.

00:05:15.926 --> 00:05:19.406
And then, there's some more
advanced applications that

00:05:19.406 --> 00:05:20.796
put various things together.

00:05:20.796 --> 00:05:24.566
So here is a Wordle application
that's written for Shiny.

00:05:24.566 --> 00:05:27.476
This is one that can use-- if
you load this on your phone,

00:05:27.476 --> 00:05:30.324
you can use your phone
camera for input,

00:05:30.324 --> 00:05:31.616
and it can do image processing.

00:05:31.616 --> 00:05:34.948
So it lets you explore different
types of visual illusions

00:05:34.948 --> 00:05:36.156
and play with the parameters.

00:05:36.156 --> 00:05:38.276
This is using a
Python package called

00:05:38.276 --> 00:05:40.746
Python Illusion, and so on.

00:05:40.746 --> 00:05:43.776
So there's all these different
types of examples over here.

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:45.574
Now, let's try to
